WEBVTT

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:14.700
<v Ira Glass>People really were different back in the '60s. Here is something that it is impossible to imagine any politician saying today-- any successful, mainstream, big-time politician anyway. Listen to the President of the United States, June 4, 1965.
<v Ira Glass>在60年代，人们确实与众不同。这里有一段话，放在今天，你不可能想象任何政客会说出口——至少任何成功的、主流的、重量级的政客都不会。请听1965年6月4日，美国总统的一段发言。

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:39.110
<v President Lyndon Baines Johnson>Perhaps most important is the breakdown of the Negro family structure. For this, most of all white America must accept responsibility. It flows from centuries years of oppression and persecution of the Negro man.
<v President Lyndon Baines Johnson>也许最重要的问题是黑人家庭结构的瓦解。对于这一点，美国白人必须承担主要责任。这是数世纪以来对黑人男性的压迫和迫害所导致的后果。

00:00:39.110 --> 00:01:00.190
<v Ira Glass>In 1965, black single mothers became a national political issue, a national symbol, for the very first time. And it was the Democrats who put the issue on the table. A young Assistant Secretary of Labor named Daniel Patrick Moynihan submitted a report to President Lyndon Johnson calling for national action, to do something about the large number of black single mothers.
<v Ira Glass>1965年，黑人单身母亲首次成为一个全国性的政治议题和一个国家象征。将这个问题摆上台面的正是民主党人。一位名叫丹尼尔·帕特里克·莫伊尼汉的年轻劳工部助理部长向林登·约翰逊总统提交了一份报告，呼吁采取全国性行动，去解决大量的黑人单身母亲问题。

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:30.790
<v Ira Glass>When large numbers of men grow up in homes without strong male authority, the report said, it leads to chaos, crime, violence, and unrest. Black single mothers, the report declared, were at the center of quote "a tangle of pathology in the black community." Black leaders, including the Reverend Doctor Martin Luther King, Junior, did not take too kindly to all this. They said that all this talk of black pathology had unfortunate racist overtones, and the issue dropped from the national scene for years.
<v Ira Glass>报告称，当大量男性在没有强大男性权威的家庭中成长时，会导致混乱、犯罪、暴力和不安。报告宣称，黑人单身母亲处于所谓的“黑人社区病态纠葛”的中心。包括马丁·路德·金博士在内的黑人领袖对这一切并不买账。他们表示，所有关于黑人病态的言论都带有令人遗憾的种族主义色彩，此后多年，这一议题便从国家舞台上消失了。

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:51.880
<v Ira Glass>Then the Republicans picked it up. Ronald Reagan talked about welfare queens. Dan Quayle said the LA riots happened mainly because of a breakdown in traditional family structure. For two decades, the entire national debate over welfare reform has often seemed like it's really just a referendum on inner city single mothers, which brings us to Barbara Clinkscales.
<v Ira Glass>后来，共和党人重新拾起了这个话题。罗纳德·里根谈到了“福利女王”。丹·奎尔说，洛杉矶骚乱主要是因为传统家庭结构的瓦解。二十年来，整个关于福利改革的国家辩论，看起来往往就像是对内城单身母亲的一场全民公投，这就引出了芭芭拉·克林克斯凯尔斯。

00:01:51.880 --> 00:02:23.980
<v Ira Glass>We gave Barbara Clinkscales a cassette recorder. And for seven months, she taped her family's life. And the story we bring you today, her life in her own words, it isn't that it completely contradicts everything in the national debate over single mothers, though sometimes it does contradict that debate. It's more that when you hear her talk, the national policy discussion somehow seems irrelevant and off the point. It's as if the policymakers have no real picture of what a life like hers is really like.
<v Ira Glass>我们给了芭芭拉·克林克斯凯尔斯一台卡式录音机。在七个月的时间里，她录下了家人的生活。今天我们为您带来的这个故事，即她用自己的话讲述的生活，并不是说它完全反驳了关于单身母亲的国家辩论，尽管有时确实如此。更多时候，当你听她诉说时，那种全国性的政策讨论似乎变得无关紧要，甚至偏离了重点。就好像决策者们根本无法想象她那样的生活到底是什么样子的。

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:39.050
<v Ira Glass>Today, we devote our entire program to her story. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>今天，我们的全部节目都将贡献给她的故事。这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台和国际公共广播电台联合制作的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:02:39.050 --> 00:03:03.630
<v Ira Glass>Like most African-American single mothers, like 77% of them, Barbara Clinkscales works for a living. She has a job in the new information economy as a data entry operator. And she's so committed to seeing that her kids graduate high school that when her son, Gerald, cut some classes, she started escorting him to class herself, telling his classmates, "My name is Barbara. I think we'll be seeing a lot of each other from now on."
<v Ira Glass>像大多数非裔美国单身母亲一样——大约占77%——芭芭拉·克林克斯凯尔斯靠工作养家糊口。她在新的信息经济领域拥有一份工作，担任数据录入员。她非常致力于确保孩子们高中毕业，以至于当她的儿子杰拉尔德旷课时，她开始亲自护送他去上课，并对他的同学说：“我叫芭芭拉。我想从现在起，我们会经常见面的。”

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:38.940
<v Ira Glass>Before we start her story, some quick statistics to put all this in perspective. Barbara's family is not unusual at all. There has been a radical shift in American family structure. The number of children born to black single mothers has risen from 17% in 1950 to 70%, seven zero, in 1994. The number of white children born to single mothers has risen even faster, from 1.7% in 1950 to 25% in 1994. Here, then, is the story of one single mother told in her own words.
<v Ira Glass>在开始她的故事之前，先提供一些简短的统计数据来衡量这一切。芭芭拉的家庭一点也不特殊。美国家庭结构发生了根本性的转变。黑人单身母亲所生子女的比例已从1950年的17%上升到1994年的70%（七零）。白人单身母亲所生子女的比例上升得更快，从1950年的1.7%上升到1994年的25%。现在，这是一个单身母亲用她自己的话讲述的故事。

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:45.870
<v Barbara Clinkscales>OK. Hi, I know we late. I'm sorry. Yeah, it's my fault this time.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>好的。嗨，我知道我们迟到了。我很抱歉。是的，这次是我的错。

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.390
<v Ira Glass>Let me introduce myself. My name is Barbara.
<v Ira Glass>让我自我介绍一下。我的名字是芭芭拉。

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.740
<v Woman At High School>Good morning, Miss Clinkscales.
<v Woman At High School>早上好，克林克斯凯尔斯小姐。

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.100
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Hi baby, good morning.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>嗨宝贝，早上好。

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:55.230
<v Ira Glass>I'm a single mom. I have three kids, Angie, Tess, and Gerald.
<v Ira Glass>我是一名单亲妈妈。我有三个孩子：安吉、泰丝和杰拉尔德。

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:55.440
<v Man At High School>Good morning.
<v Man At High School>早上好。

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.770
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Good morning.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>早上好。

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:02.870
<v Ira Glass>Angie's 25. Tess and Gerald are 17. They are twins. They're in high school, in the 11th grade.
<v Ira Glass>安吉25岁。泰丝和杰拉尔德17岁。他们是双胞胎，在读高中十一年级。

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.990
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Hi, my name is Barbara Clinkscales. Everybody knows me.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>嗨，我叫芭芭拉·克林克斯凯尔斯。每个人都认识我。

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:06.790
<v Mr. Maybaum>Yeah, I know that name from somewhere, Clinkscales.
<v Mr. Maybaum>是的，我在某个地方听说过这个名字，克林克斯凯尔斯。

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:12.520
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Yeah, I always bring him to school. And I stand here while he goes up to his locker, and he comes back.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>是的，我总是送他上学。我站在这里等他去储物柜拿东西，然后再回来。

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:42.010
<v Ira Glass>Every morning, I take Gerald to school. If I don't take him to school, he'd get up, get dressed, and he'd just sit there. He won't go. In order to take him to school, I have to go a half hour out of my way in the opposite direction of my workplace. Then after I leave his school, it takes me another 50 minutes to an hour back past my house before I get to work. Altogether, it takes me an hour and a half to two hours to get to work in the morning.
<v Ira Glass>每天早上，我都送杰拉尔德上学。如果我不送他，他起来穿好衣服后就只会坐在那儿。他不去上学。为了送他，我得绕道，比去单位的方向多花半小时。送完他后再去上班，还要再绕过我家，再花50分钟到一个小时。总之，每天早上我得花一个半到两个小时才能到单位。

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:43.270
<v Barbara Clinkscales>But I'm sorry, Mr. Maybaum.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>但我很抱歉，梅鲍姆先生。

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:52.270
<v Mr. Maybaum>That's OK, everyone's right out here. Last week, almost every afternoon-- I'm going to go get my book-- he did not come to my afternoon classes. Let me go get my book.
<v Mr. Maybaum>没关系，大家都站在外面呢。上周，几乎每天下午——我去拿我的书——他都没有来上我的下午课。让我去拿书。

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:59.730
<v Barbara Clinkscales>OK. Where were you? Where were you?
<v Barbara Clinkscales>好的。你去哪儿了？你去哪儿了？

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:00.740
<v Gerald Clinkscales>I was at school.
<v Gerald Clinkscales>我在学校里。

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.810
<v Barbara Clinkscales>You stayed in school, but you just didn't go to his class?
<v Barbara Clinkscales>你待在学校里，只是没去上他的课？

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:10.170
<v Mr. Maybaum>That doesn't sound like it makes any sense, does it, Gerald? Where did you go if you stayed in school?
<v Mr. Maybaum>这听起来说不通，对吧，杰拉尔德？如果你待在学校里，你去哪儿了？

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:29.070
<v Barbara Clinkscales>The twins don't go to school in the neighborhood we live in. I had to borrow my coworker's address to get them into Bogan High School because I feel Bogan High School is a real good school. They really care about the kids, and they work with you. Like Gerald's teacher, Mr. Maybaum. He cares about Gerald as if Gerald was his own child.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>双胞胎并不在我们居住的社区上学。我不得不借用同事的地址让他们进入博根高中，因为我觉得博根高中是一所真正的好学校。他们真心关心孩子，而且配合家长。比如杰拉尔德的老师，梅鲍姆先生，他像关心自己的孩子一样关心杰拉尔德。

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:34.540
<v Mr. Maybaum>Where have you been going? Come on. You might as well tell us. No big, dark secret, is it? Where you been going?
<v Mr. Maybaum>你一直去哪儿了？过来。你最好告诉我们。这不是什么不可告人的大秘密，是吧？你一直去哪儿了？

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:40.710
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Where did you go last week? Gerald, are those hickies on your neck?
<v Barbara Clinkscales>你上周去哪儿了？杰拉尔德，你脖子上那是吻痕吗？

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:41.200
<v Gerald Clinkscales>No.
<v Gerald Clinkscales>没有。

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.880
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Yes, they are. What a-- Turn around. You see that, Mr. Maybaum?
<v Barbara Clinkscales>是的，就是。什么——转过身来。你看到了吗，梅鲍姆先生？

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:46.480
<v Mr. Maybaum>What's that?
<v Mr. Maybaum>那是什么？

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.820
<v Barbara Clinkscales>That's what he's probably doing. Those are the love passion marks, hickies.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>他可能就是在做这个。那些是爱的印记，吻痕。

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:52.270
<v Mr. Maybaum>Could be. Could be.
<v Mr. Maybaum>可能是。可能是。

00:05:52.270 --> 00:06:01.640
<v Barbara Clinkscales>If I hit you upside your head, you'll know what they are.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>如果我揍你的头，你就知道那是什么了。

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:10.800
<v Ira Glass>Right now, all I can focus on is the twins and getting them through high school. Get them through high school.
<v Ira Glass>现在，我唯一能专注的就是这对双胞胎，让他们高中毕业。让他们高中毕业。

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:24.470
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Now we're walking him down to the other end of the school to the counselor's office, Mr. Ware, where he'll have to reinstate Gerald because of those two cuts.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>现在我们正送他去学校另一头的辅导员办公室，也就是韦尔先生那里，他得因为那两次旷课恢复杰拉尔德的资格。

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:47.180
<v Ira Glass>When I talk to them about graduating, I always tell them, when they walk across that stage, I'm going to do a cartwheel. And my best girlfriend, she says, she's going to do a split. And the twins look at us and laugh. And they say, they want to see this. And I say, well, I better start practicing because I know you guys are going to walk across that stage.
<v Ira Glass>当我和他们谈论毕业时，我总是告诉他们，当他们走上那个舞台时，我会做一个侧手翻。我最好的闺蜜说，她要做一个劈叉。双胞胎看着我们大笑，说他们想看。我说，好吧，我最好开始练习了，因为我知道你们肯定会走上那个舞台。

00:06:47.180 --> 00:07:01.250
<v Barbara Clinkscales>OK, what I was doing, that moment of silence, I was writing my name and the time, signing in, on the sign-in sheet in Room 102. I'm sitting here next to Gerald waiting.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>好了，我刚才在做什么呢，在那默哀时刻，我在102教室的签到表上写下我的名字和时间。我现在正坐在杰拉尔德旁边等着。

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:48.100
<v Ira Glass>I always dreamed about going on a prom, all the senior activities that the seniors do when they graduate from high school. I didn't get a chance to go on a prom because when I was graduating from high school, I had my own apartment. I had been out on my own ever since I was 16 years old. So I had to play grown-up. But I can live some of my dreams through them. That's why I feel the way that I feel, that nothing is going to get in my way, nothing.
<v Ira Glass>我一直梦想着参加舞会，参加所有高中毕业生参加的活动。我没有机会参加舞会，因为我高中毕业时已经自己住公寓了。从16岁起我就独立生活了，所以我不得不装作大人。但我可以通过他们来实现我的一些梦想。这就是我感觉的原因，没有什么能阻挡我，什么都没有。

00:07:48.100 --> 00:08:03.260
<v Barbara Clinkscales>You really think you're smart, Gerald, don't you? You really think you're getting away with something, don't you? Do you think I'm going to give up? Huh? Do you think I'm going to give up? Gerald, I'm talking to you.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>你真觉得自己很聪明，杰拉尔德，对吧？你真觉得你能瞒天过海，对吧？你以为我会放弃吗？哈？你以为我会放弃吗？杰拉尔德，我在跟你说话。

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:33.060
<v Barbara Clinkscales>I'm going to kill you, Gerald, and sit up in jail before I let you be out there on them streets, no education, a dymie, trying to deal drugs to have some money. I will take your life and sit up in jail gladly. Because I'm not going to live day to day, knowing my only son is a failure. All right?
<v Barbara Clinkscales>杰拉尔德，我宁愿杀了你，然后坐在监狱里，也不会让你流落街头，没受过教育，像个小混混，试图兜售毒品赚点钱。我会夺走你的生命，然后心甘情愿地坐牢。因为我不想一天天地过日子，还要知道我唯一的儿子是个失败者。行吗？

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:39.550
<v Barbara Clinkscales>[SOUND OF A BASS LINE AND GLASSES CLINKING]
<v Barbara Clinkscales>[低音伴奏和玻璃杯碰撞声]

00:08:39.550 --> 00:09:12.710
<v Ira Glass>My landlord is playing his music. I'm standing here just holding the mic. I have a portable bar because I like to collect pretty glasses. And that's just how the music is vibrating through the floor. Really, noise don't bother me. When you grow up in the projects, noise just don't bother you. You can live with it.
<v Ira Glass>我的房东在放音乐。我站在这儿拿着麦克风。我有一个移动酒吧，因为我喜欢收集漂亮的杯子。这就是音乐透过地板震动的声音。真的，噪音困扰不了我。当你成长在廉租房社区时，噪音根本不会打扰你。你能适应它。

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:29.180
<v Ira Glass>And my apartment is real big. I have three bedrooms, living room, dining room, a nice long hallway. And I'm walking into Gerald's room now. And you get-- why? I can't come in here?
<v Ira Glass>我的公寓真的很大。有三间卧室，客厅，餐厅，一条漂亮的走廊。我现在正走进杰拉尔德的房间。你弄——为什么？我不能进来吗？

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.990
<v Gerald Clinkscales>What you putting on the radio?
<v Gerald Clinkscales>你在收音机上放什么？

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:33.880
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Well, they can't see you.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>好吧，他们看不见你。

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:36.260
<v Gerald Clinkscales>But it's going to be on the radio, though.
<v Gerald Clinkscales>但这会被广播出去的。

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:40.490
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Well, you should keep it clean if you don't want nobody to know your room is dirty.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>好吧，如果你不想让别人知道你的房间很乱，你就应该保持整洁。

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:43.520
<v Gerald Clinkscales>People will be hearing it?
<v Gerald Clinkscales>人们会听到它吗？

00:09:43.520 --> 00:10:06.610
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Yes. Everything we talk about from now on, your whole life, is going to be on the radio. People are going to know how you don't want to go to school, how your momma have to take you to school every day, watch you go in your classroom.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>是的。从现在开始，我们谈论的一切，你整个生活，都会在广播里播出。人们会知道你多么不想上学，你妈妈不得不每天送你去学校，看着你走进教室。

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:29.490
<v Ira Glass>So I'm in my living room now. And I'm having company tomorrow, so we going to clean up tonight. And I can't wait for Tess to come home because I worry about her being out at night. But I can't stop her from doing the things that she want to do. But I really don't have no peace until she walk through that door. And then I feel better.
<v Ira Glass>我现在在客厅里。明天我有客人，所以我们今晚得打扫干净。我迫不及待地想让泰丝回家，因为我担心她晚上在外面。但我无法阻止她做她想做的事。但在她走进那扇门之前，我真的无法安宁。然后我才会感觉好些。

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:38.780
<v Ira Glass>OK. I guess I'll start cooking them tacos. I promised that I'd cook tacos for dinner.
<v Ira Glass>好吧。我想我该开始做墨西哥夹饼了。我答应过晚餐做夹饼的。

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:40.670
<v Ira Glass>[SOUND OF THREE GUNSHOTS]
<v Ira Glass>[三声枪响]

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:48.280
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Oh god, did you just hear that? They're shooting. Oh god, don't let that be Tess. Oh god, I hope Tess is not outside. Gerald, get away from the windows.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>哦天哪，你刚听到了吗？他们在开枪。哦上帝，别让那是泰丝。哦天哪，我希望泰丝不在外面。杰拉尔德，远离窗户。

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:49.850
<v Barbara Clinkscales>[SOUND OF TWO GUNSHOTS]
<v Barbara Clinkscales>[两声枪响]

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:51.670
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Gerald, just come in the hall. Come in the hallway, Gerald.
<v Barbara Clinkscales>杰拉尔德，快到走廊里来。到走廊里来，杰拉尔德。

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:52.950
<v Barbara Clinkscales>[SOUND OF THREE GUNSHOTS]
<v Barbara Clinkscales>[三声枪响]

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:53.370
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Gerald?
<v Barbara Clinkscales>杰拉尔德？

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:53.800
<v Gerald Clinkscales>What?
<v Gerald Clinkscales>什么？

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:55.670
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Where are you at?
<v Barbara Clinkscales>你在哪儿？

00:10:55.670 --> 00:11:13.330
<v Barbara Clinkscales>Oh god, I hope Tess is not out there. Oh god. Crime and drugs is everywhere, but I know my block is the crack block. Just wait, Gerald. Wait 'til the shooting stops.
